                                "sense_id": "094e76c4-9b47-30db-b4a0-89d80de1a38e",
                                "text": "tales of goblinry"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d6ac500-190f-4a65-9ac1-d1af73b26c32",
                                "sense_id": "094e76c4-9b47-30db-b4a0-89d80de1a38e",
                                "text": "1830, Walter Scott, The Doom of Devorgoil Act III, Scene 1, in Lyrics, Dramas and Miscellaneous Pieces, Edinburgh: Adam and Charles Black, 1875, p. 276,\nBut is there nothing, then, save rank imposture, / In all these tales of goblinry at Devorgoil?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e8b2af4-87f3-4b2f-a352-8a0aa4efc097",
                                "sense_id": "094e76c4-9b47-30db-b4a0-89d80de1a38e",
                                "text": "These [illuminated shrines] are good ghostly company for him;— […] they appear to cheer him voicelessly as he strides from gloom to gloom, under the goblinry of those woods which tower black as ebony under the stars...."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e152cbf9-6c5e-4975-87a5-32d5470057c5",
                                "sense_id": "094e76c4-9b47-30db-b4a0-89d80de1a38e",
                                "text": "There were quaint paths, pillared with antic trees, latticed with drolly peering faces of extravagant orchids, that led the seeker to hidden, surprising bowers of goblinry."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
